messages.content:
{
 "what_you_observe": [
  "Tuesday, February 2. Seven pm.",
  "Phil said in the lobby: I'm heading to the terminal. Coach to Pittsburgh. Leave it at that. I've got a door to be at.",
  "Rita records Alma at the Rialto and puts her recording on the patch.",
  "Larry said in the lobby: I say to whoever's still at the table: 'I'm taking the Rialto. You guys cover the ballroom tonight.' I don't wait for an answer. I'm already at the door with the bag over my shoulder.",
  "Larry records Alma on his last charged take. The reel stays with him.",
  "You are now at Rialto.",
  "There is time for one evening visit. The ballroom lights are on and the station line is open."
 ],
 "your_own_memories": [
  {
   "what_you_did": "Rita records Alma at the Rialto and puts her recording on the patch.",
   "what_you_said": "Ballroom's yours. I'm on the Rialto. One mic, one cable, a chair if her back's bad. I'll handle the rest.",
   "your_intention": "Be in the Rialto booth before the last reel loads onto the regional line. Sit beside Alma, not across. Let her talk. Record it. Send the file to Pittsburgh tonight with no title, no slug, no broadcast package. One voice, one daughter, one machine about to stop."
  }
 ],
 "who_you_are_with": "your present location",
 "physical_choices": {
  "transmit_interview": "Take the regional line at the station and send any usable interview with Alma to her daughter.",
  "go_dancing": "Spend the evening at the ballroom, open to dancing with a colleague.",
  "go_to_party_for_work": "Cover the party with your notebook, without dancing.",
  "take_evening_off": "Choose a quiet evening on your own."
 }
}

{
  "action": "transmit_interview",
  "detail": "I'm still in the booth. The chair's got the dip in it where I sat beside her. My tape's on the patch, already spliced in. The station line's open, the little green lamp's steady, Pittsburgh is a number away. I reach over, lift the handset off the cradle, and route my reel straight onto the regional feed. No slug. No intro. No 'this is a production by.' I press the talk key, let the line go live, and I play the tape. Her voice comes up out of the little speaker in the booth, thin and close, the projector whirring under it the way it was when she was sitting here telling me about the carbons, the 1962 summer, the time she ran a reel by hand because the motor quit and she didn't want to stop the picture. I don't touch the faders. I don't ride the levels. I let it be what it is. One voice. A machine humming under it. I sit with my hands flat on the desk and I watch the counter tick. When the last frame of audio runs off the end of the tape and the little motor clicks to a stop, I leave the line open for a few seconds. The silence on the wire is the silence in the booth. Then I set the handset back. The green lamp goes dark. I don't write a log entry. I don't call the ballroom. I don't call Phil at the terminal. I sit in the chair with the dip in it for a minute, and then I turn off the booth light and leave the key on the hook where the next person will find it. The reel is on the patch. The line held. She's in Pittsburgh and she's got her mother's voice. That's the piece. That was always the piece. I don't need the ballroom. I don't need the notebook. I don't need to cover anything. The evening is done. I walk out of the Rialto into the February air and I don't look back at the building.","intention": "Send Alma's voice to her daughter in Pittsburgh tonight, unedited, unslugged, unframed as content. One recording. One wire. One person on the other end who was promised a phone call that would actually be her mother talking. The machine is about to stop. The reel is what's left. I put it through the line and I let it be enough.","words": "Nothing. I don't say a word to the booth, to the tape, to the empty room. The voice on the wire is the only voice. I let it go. I let the silence after it go. I don't need to fill it."
}
